\chapter{Renderer}

The rope is drawn as one continuous tube, not as a row of spheres or a line.
The tube follows a smooth curve through the link centres, so it reads as a rope.
The renderer owns no physics. It reads a snapshot and writes geometry.

The tube's vertex buffer is allocated once, when the renderer is built. Each
frame the same buffer is rewritten in place, so the frame loop allocates no new
geometry. That follows the three.js manual, which says a geometry is not freed
automatically and that a buffer should be reused when its layout is fixed.
Three.js itself allocates small temporaries when it evaluates the curve, but it
does not reallocate the buffer.

\section{The tube}

Each frame the link centres become the control points of a Catmull-Rom curve.
Along the curve the renderer places a ring of vertices, perpendicular to the
tangent. The ring's orientation is carried from one ring to the next by
projecting the previous normal onto the new tangent plane. This keeps the tube
from twisting as the curve bends. The first ring starts from an arbitrary
perpendicular.

The buffer's layout depends only on the link count and the radial resolution,
both fixed at construction. The index buffer is built once and never changes.

@d Renderer: rope tube @{
import * as THREE from 'three';
import type { RopeSnapshot } from '../rope/snapshot';

const RADIAL = 8;
const SAMPLES_PER_LINK = 4;

// One tube along the rope. The buffers are allocated once in the constructor, and
// update() rewrites the vertex positions in place.
export class RopeTube {
  readonly mesh: THREE.Mesh;
  readonly geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
  private readonly material = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
    color: 0xd9a441,
    roughness: 0.6,
    side: THREE.DoubleSide,
  });
  private readonly positions: Float32Array;
  private readonly attribute: THREE.BufferAttribute;
  private readonly controls: THREE.Vector3[];
  private readonly curve: THREE.CatmullRomCurve3;
  private readonly rings: number;
  private readonly radius: number;
  private readonly center = new THREE.Vector3();
  private readonly tangent = new THREE.Vector3();
  private readonly normal = new THREE.Vector3();
  private readonly binormal = new THREE.Vector3();
  private readonly seed = new THREE.Vector3();

  constructor(linkCount: number, radius: number) {
    this.radius = radius;
    this.rings = (linkCount - 1) * SAMPLES_PER_LINK + 1;

    this.positions = new Float32Array(this.rings * (RADIAL + 1) * 3);
    this.attribute = new THREE.BufferAttribute(this.positions, 3);
    this.geometry.setAttribute('position', this.attribute);

    const indices: number[] = [];
    for (let r = 0; r < this.rings - 1; r++) {
      for (let s = 0; s < RADIAL; s++) {
        const a = r * (RADIAL + 1) + s;
        const c = a + (RADIAL + 1);
        indices.push(a, c, a + 1, a + 1, c, c + 1);
      }
    }
    this.geometry.setIndex(indices);

    this.controls = Array.from({ length: linkCount }, () => new THREE.Vector3());
    this.curve = new THREE.CatmullRomCurve3(this.controls);
    this.mesh = new THREE.Mesh(this.geometry, this.material);
    // The bounds are not recomputed each frame, so culling would use stale ones.
    this.mesh.frustumCulled = false;
  }

  update(snapshot: RopeSnapshot): void {
    snapshot.points.forEach((p, i) => this.controls[i].set(p.x, p.y, p.z));
    this.curve.points = this.controls;

    for (let r = 0; r < this.rings; r++) {
      const t = r / (this.rings - 1);
      this.curve.getPoint(t, this.center);
      this.curve.getTangent(t, this.tangent);
      if (r === 0) {
        // Start from any direction that is not along the tangent.
        this.seed.set(Math.abs(this.tangent.x) > 0.9 ? 0 : 1, Math.abs(this.tangent.x) > 0.9 ? 1 : 0, 0);
        this.normal.copy(this.seed);
      }
      // Project the carried normal onto the plane perpendicular to the tangent.
      this.normal.addScaledVector(this.tangent, -this.normal.dot(this.tangent)).normalize();
      this.binormal.crossVectors(this.tangent, this.normal);

      for (let s = 0; s <= RADIAL; s++) {
        const angle = (s / RADIAL) * Math.PI * 2;
        const cos = Math.cos(angle) * this.radius;
        const sin = Math.sin(angle) * this.radius;
        const o = (r * (RADIAL + 1) + s) * 3;
        this.positions[o] = this.center.x + this.normal.x * cos + this.binormal.x * sin;
        this.positions[o + 1] = this.center.y + this.normal.y * cos + this.binormal.y * sin;
        this.positions[o + 2] = this.center.z + this.normal.z * cos + this.binormal.z * sin;
      }
    }
    this.attribute.needsUpdate = true;
  }

  dispose(): void {
    this.geometry.dispose();
    this.material.dispose();
  }
}
@}

\section{Tests}

The renderer is tested without a browser. A straight rope is the simplest case:
every vertex must lie exactly one radius from the rope's axis. The test also
checks that the buffer is the same object before and after an update, which is
the no-allocation promise made above.

@d Renderer: tube tests @{
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import type { RopeSnapshot } from '../rope/snapshot';
import { RopeTube } from './ropeTube';

const LINKS = 40;
const RADIUS = 0.02;

const straight: RopeSnapshot = {
  radius: RADIUS,
  points: Array.from({ length: LINKS }, (_, i) => ({ x: 0, y: -i * 0.05, z: 0 })),
};

describe('rope tube', () => {
  it('keeps the same vertex buffer across updates', () => {
    const tube = new RopeTube(LINKS, RADIUS);
    const before = tube.geometry.getAttribute('position').array;
    tube.update(straight);
    tube.update(straight);
    expect(tube.geometry.getAttribute('position').array).toBe(before);
    tube.dispose();
  });

  it('places every vertex one radius from the rope axis', () => {
    const tube = new RopeTube(LINKS, RADIUS);
    tube.update(straight);
    const array = tube.geometry.getAttribute('position').array;
    for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i += 3) {
      const distance = Math.hypot(array[i], array[i + 2]);
      expect(distance).toBeCloseTo(RADIUS, 6);
    }
    tube.dispose();
  });
});
@}
